KINGDOMS
--------
Guild territory on the overhead map

Syntax:   kingdom <subcommand>

A guild that has raised a guildhall may go further and become a KINGDOM:
a guild that holds land. Territory is measured in map squares, it is
claimed one square at a time, it costs coin to take and coin to keep, and
it can be lost again if the treasury runs dry.

Kingdoms are an optional system. If your server has not enabled them the
command will say so, and nothing below applies.


THE SHAPE OF A REALM
--------------------
A realm is not drawn freehand. It grows outward from the guildhall
entrance in four concentric rings:

        ring 1     3x3      8 squares
        ring 2     5x5     16 squares
        ring 3     7x7     24 squares
        ring 4     9x9     32 squares

That is 80 claimable squares, plus the hall's own square in the middle -
81 in all, a nine-by-nine block with the hall at its heart.

The order is fixed. Within a ring, land is taken CLOCKWISE beginning due
NORTH of the hall, and a ring must be finished before the next one opens.
You cannot skip ahead, reach for a corner, or take a square out of turn.
'kingdom claim' always takes the next square in the sequence, and
'kingdom map' shows you which one that is.

                77 78 79 80 49 50 51 52 53
                76 46 47 48 25 26 27 28 54
                75 45 23 24  9 10 11 29 55
                74 44 22  8  1  2 12 30 56
                73 43 21  7  @  3 13 31 57
                72 42 20  6  5  4 14 32 58
                71 41 19 18 17 16 15 33 59
                70 40 39 38 37 36 35 34 60
                69 68 67 66 65 64 63 62 61

The @ is your hall. The numbers are the order the squares are taken in.


WHERE A REALM MAY STAND
-----------------------
Every one of the 80 squares must be legal ground BEFORE THE GUILDHALL IS
BUILT - not later, when you get round to claiming it. A hall may only be
raised where a complete realm could one day fit, so a site that suits the
hall itself can still be refused because square sixty-one falls in open
water or off the edge of the map.

The rules, each checked against every square:

  - There must be a room there. Much of the world map is empty space
    with nothing behind it, and empty space cannot be claimed.
  - The ground must be settleable. Open water and impassable terrain
    are out.
  - THE UNDERDARK IS CLOSED TO REALMS ENTIRELY, for every race and
    every alignment. There are no underground kingdoms.
  - Not too close to a hometown, so a realm cannot wall a city in.
  - Not too close to a zone entrance, so a realm cannot bottle up the
    way into an area.
  - Clear of every other realm. Two realms may not even touch: there is
    always at least one unclaimed square between them.

'kingdom survey' judges the ground you are standing on and reports,
square by square, exactly what would refuse you.


TAKING AND LOSING LAND
----------------------
Claiming costs coin from the guild treasury, and each square costs more
than the one before it, so the outer ring is far dearer than the first.
The prices are set by your server.

Holding land costs coin as well. Upkeep is charged on every square you
own, every cycle. If the treasury cannot pay, the realm decays in this
order:

  1. The guards go home.
  2. The harvest nodes fall dormant.
  3. One OUTER RING is lost per further missed payment.

Paying stops the slide wherever it has reached, and the guards and the
nodes come back. LAND ALREADY LOST DOES NOT RETURN ON PAYMENT - a ring
that has reverted must be claimed again from scratch, at full price.

'kingdom abandon' gives up your most recent square voluntarily.
Territory always stays contiguous, so it is always the last square taken
that goes first.


GUARDS
------
A realm may keep guards in proportion to its size - one for every few
squares held - so a full realm supports a real garrison and a young one
supports very little. They stand on your land. They do not follow you
and they are not a bodyguard.


RESOURCES
---------
Owned squares carry harvest nodes, and what a node yields depends on the
ground it stands on. What you gather goes into the realm's own stores.

BE CLEAR ABOUT THIS: RESOURCES PUT INTO A REALM CANNOT BE TAKEN OUT
AGAIN. They are not the guild bank and they are not spending money. They
exist to be spent on the realm itself, and once given they are given.

Nodes lie dormant while upkeep is unpaid.


SUBCOMMANDS
-----------
kingdom            - with no argument, your realm's status.
kingdom status     - territory, rings, treasury standing, guards, stores.
kingdom map        - the nine-by-nine grid: what you hold, what is next,
                     and what is blocked.
kingdom survey     - judge the ground you are standing on as a site for a
                     realm, square by square, before committing to it.
kingdom convert    - turn your guild into a kingdom. This grants NO land,
                     only the right to start claiming it.
kingdom claim      - take the next square in the sequence.
kingdom abandon    - give up your most recent square.
kingdom harvest    - work the node on the square you are standing on.
kingdom deposit    - put gathered resources into the realm's stores.
kingdom guards     - review the garrison.
kingdom help       - this text.

'kingdom c' and 'kingdom s' are ambiguous - c is shared by claim and
convert, s by status and survey - and will ask which you meant rather
than guess, because each pair contains something that spends money.

